# Notes

These need to be added to a real doc, but for now I don't want to lose track
of them.

- When a "format" is registered, (RegisterFormat(NAME, CHECKER{})), the
  NAME is a regexp to it can accept any number of variants. E.g. `jsonSchem.*`.
- talk about structure of the server
  - supports multiple registries & default registry
  - supports a UI that can be used against any registry (local or remote)
- talk about the conformance checker
- talk about CLI (xr)
  - features
  - limitations - when to use flags vs raw json
- talk about /xregs/XXX and /xreg paths. /xreg/XXX for any Reg, / and /xreg for
  default Reg
- talk about ..../ui to get to the UI
- talk about --root
- .xr
  - header.foo: VALUE
  - server.url: URL
- .xrserver
  - rootapp: ui   allowed: ui|xreg
  - ui.dir: ""
  - ui.xrui.json: PATH
  - verbose: 0
  - http.port: 8080
  - db.name: registry
  - db.host: localhost
  - db.port: 3306
  - db.user: root
  - db.password: password
  - path.ui: ui
  - path.defaultreg: xreg
  - path.regcollection: xregs
- xr env vars
  - XR_VERBOSE
  - XR_SERVER
  - XR_SHOWLOGS: false
- xrserver env vars
  - XR_PORT -> http.port
  - DBNAME -> db.name
  - DBHOST -> db.host
  - DBPORT -> db.port
  - DBUSER -> db.user
  - DBPASSWORD -> db.password
- setting up the server
  - easy path
  - setup https, auth, ... - e.g. nginx as a front-end
  - via docker - all in one & separate DB
    - persistent volumes for the DB
- How to use the UI
  - JSON vs list view
  - Viewing/hiding xR data
  - adding servers
  - finding servers
  - xrui.json - see registry/ui/xrui.json for fields
